[Cheonji Ilbo Gwangyang = Reporter Lee Bong-hwa] Gwangyang, South Jeolla Province, is strengthening its pregnancy and childbirth support measures to combat low birth rates, having recorded population growth for three consecutive years from 2022 to 2024.&nbsp;

Starting this year, the city is expanding support for pregnancy and childbirth medical expenses and postpartum care costs, along with various policies including health checkups for newlyweds.&nbsp;

The eligibility criteria for birth congratulatory funds have also been relaxed, reducing the residency requirement for parents to six months and extending the application period to one year.&nbsp;

Additionally, a public postpartum care center is under construction, set to be completed in October.&nbsp;

All these measures aim to reduce the economic burden on families and create a stable environment for raising children.

